  "body": " From plane curves to normal polynomials  In the plane proof we work over and write . The reduction and normal specialization below follow .   Universal systems  For positive integers , let mean that, for every tuple in , some nonzero form of degree has multiplicity at least at every marked point. The form may depend on the tuple.   For each , there is one countable union of proper closed subsets of , with nonempty complement, such that a violation of at any tuple outside implies for some positive integers with . Moreover, implies for every positive integer .   Fix a degree and a vector of nonnegative integers. The tuples supporting a nonzero degree- form with these multiplicities form a closed incidence locus . Indeed, in affine charts the Taylor coefficients of orders below form a matrix whose entries are polynomials in the coordinates of the points. The form exists exactly when this matrix has less than full column rank, so the locus is cut out by its maximal minors. These equations are defined over .  Let be the union of all such loci that are proper. There are countably many. A tuple whose affine coordinates are algebraically independent over avoids every proper locus and the diagonals, so the complement is nonempty. If a tuple outside admits a degree- form with multiplicities at least , then its incidence locus is all of . At any tuple choose a form for each cyclic permutation of the multiplicity vector and multiply the forms. The product has degree and common multiplicity at least . A violation gives and Taking powers gives the last assertion. The cyclic-product reduction is Nagata's .  The incidence locus is either excluded as proper or equals the entire configuration space. In the latter case every cyclic permutation is available at an arbitrary tuple. Each original multiplicity then contributes once at each point of the product divisor.    Thus the same exceptional set works simultaneously for all degrees and all multiplicity vectors. In the rest of the argument we assume and derive a contradiction.    Normal specialization  Let be a smooth plane curve of degree , and let and . The normal bundle of in the plane is . A polynomial section on takes values in the pullback of when . The normal-bundle identification follows from the Cartier-divisor formula in ( Tag 0B3P ).   If holds, then for any distinct and arbitrary normal vectors , there is a nonzero polynomial section of the displayed form with multiplicity at least at each .   Choose an equation of and move each along an affine line whose first normal derivative is . For , the universal property gives a degree- form vanishing to order at every . The equations for its coefficients are linear over . Their rank is deficient for every general complex , hence also over . After clearing denominators and dividing out a common power of , obtain a polynomial family with .  For each nonzero coefficient write , where . Let be the smallest value of . Rescale a local equation of by and divide the family by . Its limit is The terms have distinct exponents, so . Since has degree , the coefficients lie in the asserted spaces. The change of frame for is the transition for the normal bundle, so these local expressions glue.  A term acquires a factor after . Choosing the least value of retains the first nonzero normal limit. For other degenerations and fat-point limits, see also and .  In coordinates centered at the moving points, the rescaled family is holomorphic through . Its derivatives of total order below vanish for general and hence at . The normal coordinates of the moving points tend to . Therefore has the required multiplicities.      Square values of the number of points   If with , then is impossible whenever .   Take a smooth plane curve of degree . Its genus is positive, and . Choose distinct points such that, for , the degree-zero bundle is nontorsion. Fixing points and varying the last gives uncountably many distinct bundles, whereas the torsion subgroup of is countable. Distinctness follows because a linear equivalence between two different single points would give a degree-one map to , contradicting positive genus.  The restriction sequence from the plane gives . Choose normal vectors outside the image of the evaluation map . Let be given by , and let be its largest nonzero exponent. Since , one has . The required vanishing of is included in ( Tag 01XT ).  If the highest nonzero fiber degree were below , the equality case would give a section of a nontrivial degree-zero bundle. The nontorsion choice forces the highest degree to be . The coefficient is then compared with the chosen normal vectors.  If , differentiating times in the fiber shows that vanishes to order at least at each . The degree satisfies Strict inequality is impossible by the zero count. Equality forces and a nonzero section of , which is impossible because is nontorsion. Thus and .  Now is a nonzero constant. On the fiber at , multiplicity at forces . Comparison of the coefficients of gives . But , contradicting the choice of the vector .      The cubic bound  For the nonsquare case, take a smooth cubic and give zero normal displacement to nine points . Write . If has multiplicity at least at these nine points, then the coefficients with satisfy The coefficient of must vanish to base order at least at each point.   If and holds, then .   For , every possible exponent is below , and the displayed bundle has degree . Thus all coefficients vanish, contrary to . If , choose the nine points so that is nontorsion. Each coefficient with would be a section of and hence vanishes. The normal polynomial is then a nonzero constant times . Give a tenth point a nonzero normal displacement. This polynomial does not vanish there, again contradicting .    If is nonsquare, a hypothetical universal system with therefore has The second inequality is strict because is rational.   "
