  "body": " The elliptic coefficient spaces  The coefficient spaces and theta bases in this chapter are those of . All section spaces on the cubics may be taken to be holomorphic; the algebraic sections obtained by normal specialization belong to them.   Nine prescribed zeros  Keep nonsquare and suppose that holds with . By , . Let be a smooth plane cubic, , , and a divisor of distinct points. Let be its canonical section. Define and , and write and . Multiplication by sends to . Write for this change of fiber coordinate.  Define spaces of coefficients by The second line is an image inside , not necessarily that whole space. The polynomial sections take values in the pullback of to .  For , dividing by the prescribed zeros gives the whole coefficient space of degree . For , the coefficient retains a factor ; its dimension is that of , not that of the larger target bundle.   Fix a coordinate disk in and a frame of there. If holds, then for every choice of distinct points in that disk, a nonzero section of has multiplicity at least at all the points .  Apply with zero normal displacements at and displacements at the other points. For the resulting polynomial , the nine zero displacements give for . Therefore extends across and lies in . It is nonzero because the substitution is invertible off . Near each , the substitution and multiplication by are invertible, so they preserve multiplicity.     Sections on a multiplicative elliptic curve  For , let . For and , denote by the line bundle whose sections in the covering-space frame are holomorphic functions satisfying Tensor products add and multiply . The theta product converges locally uniformly on and satisfies . Hence is a section of with a single simple zero at the point represented by . The convention and quasiperiodicity formula are .   For , the space has dimension . If is a section, then One freely chosen coefficient in each residue class modulo gives a basis.  Compare Laurent coefficients in the functional equation. Conversely, the displayed recurrence extends each chosen coefficient in both directions. Because and , the exponents in the resulting series have a positive quadratic term in . The series converges normally on compact annuli and satisfies the functional equation. Distinct residue classes give independent sections.   The bundle has degree ; this follows from the zeros of theta products. In particular, has three sections. The same dimension calculation after imposing one or two prescribed zeros shows that its complete linear system has no base points and separates points and tangent directions. It embeds as a smooth plane cubic with . The Laurent-series description is also given in .    A family of marked cubics  Let , , and . Then , , and Choose a rational number with and . Let , and choose distinct , , such that . Define , , and . For each , embed as a plane cubic using , and let be the divisor of the nine points represented by .  The product represents . Its multiplier gives , and hence This multiplier determines the exponent ranges in the theta bases. Denote by the resulting space .  Near the point represented by , use local coordinates and . Since every , the same sufficiently small -disk avoids for all small . In the covering-space frame, a section has scalar expression For every fixed and every distinct positions sufficiently near zero, gives a nonzero of multiplicity at least at .   "
