  "title": "Collision and the limiting matrix",
  "body": " Collision and the limiting matrix  The two limiting arguments follow . The points are collided on each fixed cubic before its theta bases are compared as .   The collision on a fixed cubic  For , define the set of jet indices For a fixed , let take to the derivatives indexed by .   Under the hypothetical universal property, for every fixed .   For each positive integer , choose distinct numbers tending to zero. The last paragraph of gives a nonzero with multiplicity at least at . Fix a norm on the finite-dimensional space , normalize , and take a convergent subsequence. Its limit has norm one.  For each , the holomorphic function has a zero of order at least at every . The functions converge locally uniformly, with their derivatives, to . If this limit is nonzero, the argument principle on a small circle around zero gives ; if it vanishes identically, the same derivative conditions hold. Hence all the derivatives indexed by vanish on , so .  After fiber derivatives, each of the separate points contributes a zero of order in one variable. The resulting conditions are those of the ideal . This is a statement about specified mixed coefficients, not the addition of ordinary surface multiplicities.    The order of the limits matters. The sections in this proof are chosen after is fixed. The subsequent limit compares matrices, not a family of selected kernel vectors.    Normalized Laurent bases  Consider with and , where . Set and suppose . Each integer in the open interval represents one residue class modulo . In the Laurent recurrence of , choose its starting coefficient to be . At , the corresponding section is The exponent of is positive for , since ; it grows quadratically with . Thus this expression and each fixed derivative in tend locally uniformly to as .  For the coefficient blocks , put For , the bundle has degree and its normalized basis is indexed by the integers . For and , normalize a basis of indexed by , then multiply by . In either formula, the coefficient of in is , while the other terms are rational. Thus none of these endpoints is integral.  If divides , there is one remaining block at : it is the image of under multiplication by . We label this basis element by . Let be the union of all the pairs just described. It indexes a basis of , and  Because , the theta products satisfy locally uniformly with all fixed derivatives. The normalized basis therefore has the common limit This also holds for the degree-zero block with .    The matrix  In the basis indexed by , the matrix of has rows indexed by . The preceding convergence gives its entrywise limit If has full column rank, one of its maximal minors is nonzero. The corresponding minor of the matrix of remains nonzero for sufficiently small positive . Then is injective, contrary to . It remains to prove the rank assertion. The passage from one-point derivative matrices to polynomial evaluation on exponent diagrams appears in . The allowed degrees here are specified by the collision index set .  The columns correspond to basis sections, while the rows correspond to derivatives. For the limiting exponential, each derivative contributes a power of its exponent. Row interpolation must therefore span every function on to give full column rank.   "
