  "body": " Interpolation and completion  The row interpolation and polygon estimates are those of . Their degree bounds depend on the distribution of row sizes, rather than only on the total number of exponents.   Interpolation by rows  For the exponent set , let . The rows of are evaluations on of the monomials spanning Thus has full column rank exactly when these polynomials interpolate arbitrary values on .   Let be finite, with coordinates . Suppose every represented row has and, for each integer , Then the restrictions of give every function .   Order the represented heights as so that their row sizes are nonincreasing. The first rows have size at least , whence . Suppose values on the first rows have been matched. Put . Since and the -coordinates in row are distinct, a polynomial of degree at most can supply the required correction on this row. The polynomial vanishes on preceding rows.  Every monomial in this correction has and . Therefore the correction lies in . Repeating this step interpolates the prescribed function.      The polygon containing the exponents  Recall , , and . Let and use normalized coordinates and . The endpoint formulas for in imply where . For , these inequalities are the interval . For , they follow by substituting in the lower endpoint and its upper endpoint. The possible degree-zero block lies at the common endpoint of the two bounds.  Choose sufficiently close to one that This is possible because . Define The preceding bounds give Indeed, their lower bound is at least . If , the displayed condition on makes the upper boundary of exceed . If , subtracting from both upper bounds reduces the comparison to .  The polygon satisfies . Its unscaled form has vertices , , , and . Directly solving its two boundary equations shows that the length of its horizontal slice at height is The maximum slice length is one. After scaling by and translating by , the heights of nonempty slices of length at least , for , form an interval of length The identity is exactly the sum of the two slopes in this length calculation.    The integer row bounds   If , then the exponent set satisfies the hypotheses of .   Each horizontal slice of has length at most . The integer -positions in a row therefore number at most . A row containing at least positions requires a nonempty slice of length at least . If , there are no such rows. Otherwise their integer heights lie in an interval of length . An interval of length contains at most integers. Thus where the last inequality is precisely .  Passing from an interval length to its number of integer heights adds at most one. The strict inequality leaves a gap of size . Taking a common power increases this gap without changing or .    Under this size condition, and show that has full column rank. By , is then injective for all sufficiently small .    Completion   For every , no universal system exists with positive integers satisfying .   If is a square, gives the result. Otherwise, because is rational. By , also . Choose and as above. The implication in permits a common multiple large enough that . Taking this multiple does not change the ratio or the choices of , , and . Apply the construction to this pair.  For every fixed , gives . The row count and interpolation give full column rank of , so is injective for all sufficiently small positive . This contradiction excludes the universal system.    By , proves simultaneously for all degrees and all vectors of nonnegative multiplicities. The argument concerns every nonzero form, including those defining reducible or nonreduced curves.    The plane Seshadri constant  Let be the blowup at a tuple covered by , with the pullback of a line and the exceptional curves. The class has square zero. It intersects each exceptional curve in one and the strict transform of any plane curve in . Hence it is strictly nef. Dividing by gives the lower bound for the Seshadri constant, and gives the matching upper bound. Thus This is .    The plane inequality over other fields   Let be an uncountable algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. For every , the strict inequality of holds outside a countable union of proper closed subsets of , with nonempty complement.  The incidence loci in are defined over by minors of the matrices of Taylor coefficients. Whenever , the corresponding locus is proper over by . A nonzero rational defining minor remains nonzero under field extension, so the locus is proper over . Exclude these loci for the countably many violating pairs . Because is uncountable, it contains elements algebraically independent over : at each successive choice the algebraic closure of the field generated by the preceding elements is countable. Using them as affine coordinates gives a tuple avoiding every excluded locus and every diagonal. This proves .    "
