  "title": "",
  "body": " Let be positive integers. If the evaluation on is injective at some distinct points of , then the corresponding evaluation on is injective at some distinct points of .   Use torus coordinates , so that . For each nonempty integer slice of , let and be its least and greatest integer heights. Convexity implies that every integer between them occurs. The slice has a basis Expansion in powers of gives a triangular change of basis with diagonal entries one. By , so each pair lies in . These pairs are distinct.  Let be an injective test tuple for in coordinates . Substitute into the displayed basis and divide the member indexed by by . For positive tending to zero, the functions become Choose bounded neighborhoods of the finitely many points on which stays away from zero. For small , one has there. The displayed convergence is locally uniform, including for negative Laurent powers. The Cauchy formula gives convergence of all derivatives of order at most .  The limiting monomials span a subspace of , so their jet matrix has full column rank. By , the rescaled functions also have full column rank for sufficiently small . They form a rescaled pullback of a basis of .  The corresponding points in the original torus are They are nonzero and distinct. The coordinate map has Jacobian determinant , so it preserves orders of vanishing. The full column rank is exactly the claimed injectivity.   "
