  "body": " Finite subgroups and maximal constants on surfaces   Multiplicity in a triangle  For a bounded real set, its vertical range is the length of its projection to the second coordinate axis. The following estimate is , Lemma 4.1.   Let , and let be a triangle of area and vertical range . Every nonzero supported in satisfies .   Let be the horizontal slice length at height , and let be the vertex heights. If the heights are distinct, increases linearly from zero at to its maximum at , then decreases linearly to zero at . If two heights coincide, there is one linear segment. In both cases the area is one half the vertical range times the greatest slice length. Thus this greatest length is . For , the heights with form an interval of length . When the heights are distinct, its endpoints are The same formula applies when adjacent heights coincide.  Write with each displayed row nonzero, and let be the least order of these rows at . Factor each row as and let . Then and . The factors remain Laurent polynomials. The horizontal exponent span of is exactly plus that of , because the two extreme coefficients in the product are nonzero. Therefore for every row, and the heights in span an interval of length at most .  Specialization to can remove rows but cannot add a height. A power of , which is a unit at , turns into a polynomial of degree at most . Hence The first inequality follows because restriction cannot produce a term of smaller total degree in the Taylor series. Orders add under multiplication of nonzero germs, giving . The orders and exponent spans are integers; the vertices and need not be integral.      A subgroup of prescribed size  Continue to write and for a bounded real diagram. For , write for their oriented determinant. An integer vector is primitive if its coordinates are coprime.   Let , let be a positive integer, and let be a triangle of area . Suppose that a primitive satisfies There is a subgroup with exactly points such that, for all positive integers with , the evaluation on at is injective.   Complete to an integer basis with . Let and , a sublattice of index . For , write . Define The characters and are torus coordinates because their exponent matrix is unimodular. In these coordinates, , which has exactly distinct complex points.  Suppose that vanishes to order at least at all points of . For , let . Translation permutes and preserves local orders. For a coset , the sum retains precisely the monomials in that coset. Indeed, write . Summation over the th roots of unity gives Every has the prescribed orders and remains supported in . At least one component is nonzero.  This proof uses the distinct roots of unity and the scalar . If the characteristic divides , these steps fail and is not étale. The later proof instead uses polynomial interpolation at arbitrary distinct points.  Multiply a nonzero component by the unit . Its support is in . In coordinates and , there is a nonzero Laurent polynomial such that The map is , with derivative at the identity. It is locally biholomorphic there, and hence .  Let be the real matrix with columns . The support of is contained in . Since , its area is . The second coordinate of is , so its vertical range is . Translation by does not change either value. Apply with . It gives , a contradiction.    This is , Proposition 4.2.    A direction for every large point count  Return to the fixed surface data and the triangles of . The freedom in gives the determinant range required in .   For every sufficiently large integer , there are and a primitive such that the determinant range of is . The threshold depends only on .   Let , , and . As varies in , varies continuously and bijectively in . For sufficiently large , choose to be the largest power of two at most . Then The open interval has length , which exceeds two for all sufficiently large . It contains an odd integer . Since is a power of two, is primitive.  The strict inequalities defining give Choose the unique for which . With , one has and . The determinant values at the vertices , , and are , , and . Their minimum is and maximum is , giving range . All restrictions on depend only on the fixed data.     There is an integer such that, for each , one can choose one and one subgroup with points for which the evaluation on is injective for every pair of positive integers satisfying .  For fixed , choose by and apply . The choices of precede the choices of .   The direction and the corollary are , Lemma 4.3 and Corollary 4.4.    Homogeneous vanishing and nefness  The surface argument converts the injective tests with equal vanishing orders into an inequality for every curve. It uses positive-square divisors, which need not be nef.   Let be a smooth integral complex projective surface, let be a nef real divisor class, and let be a rational Cartier divisor. If and , then for every sufficiently large positive integer divisible by the denominators of .   For such integers, Serre duality gives For large , . An effective divisor has nonnegative intersection with , so this is zero. Surface Riemann–Roch gives for sufficiently large , because . Serre duality is ( Tag 0FVV ); the surface Riemann–Roch formula is .  Only must vanish. Then , and the positive square gives a positive quadratic term. The auxiliary divisor itself need not be nef; nefness of and exclude sections of .     Let be distinct points of , set , and suppose that, for all positive integers with , no nonzero section of vanishes to order at least at all these points. On their blow-up , with exceptional curves , the class is nef.   Choose a Cartier divisor representing and write and . The class is nef. The blowup intersection identities , , and give . If is not nef, there is an integral curve with . Since , is not exceptional, and .  For a sufficiently small positive rational , and . Fix such . Continuity gives a rational number , close enough to , such that satisfies and .  Choose a sufficiently large positive integer divisible by the denominators of and . By , there is an effective divisor . Since and are positive integers, is effective. For the blow-up of the distinct smooth points, The projection formula gives a nonzero section of vanishing to order at least at every . This contradicts . The local ideal identity for this surface blowup is ( Tag 0AGS ), part (4).  The displayed blow-up identity expresses the local multiplicity condition: a section of a pullback line bundle descends across the missing points because is normal, and the exceptional order of a local equation is its ordinary multiplicity. Thus its order along each is precisely membership in .    The positive-square argument and the nefness criterion are , Lemma 5.2 and Proposition 5.3.    Maximality at very general tuples  Fix the nodal data from and let be the threshold in . For every , that corollary gives injective torus jets for all positive integers with . By , the evaluation on is injective at some tuple of distinct surface points for each such pair.  Let be the configuration space of distinct ordered tuples. Each injectivity condition defines a nonempty Zariski-open subset by . There are countably many pairs , so the conditions hold together outside a countable union of proper closed subsets of , with nonempty complement. At every tuple in this complement, gives nefness of The volume bound gives the reverse inequality for the Seshadri constant. Therefore This proves the surface case of , namely , Theorem 1.1. The threshold depends only on ; it was fixed before the section degree and vanishing order.   "
