  "number": "11",
  "title": "Interior lattice points and interpolation",
  "body": " Interior lattice points and interpolation   Interior lattice points  The counting argument in and uses addition across degrees to determine which lattice points occur in the interior of a bounding convex set. For a more general approximation theorem for additive semigroups, see also .    Let , with , be compact and convex with nonempty interior. Suppose that finite sets , indexed by all integers , satisfy and For every compact set , there is an integer such that    The boundary of a full-dimensional compact convex set has measure zero. Lattice-point counting by Riemann sums gives Thus the sets of missing lattice points satisfy .  Assume is nonempty. Choose so that the closed ball of radius about each point of lies in . For a large integer , let and . Fix and write . Every lattice vector satisfying defines a decomposition with . Indeed, and both lie within distance of .  The ball from which is chosen contains at least lattice points for some and all sufficiently large , uniformly in its center. An inscribed cube gives this estimate. At most choices have . At most choices have , since is injective. Their sum is , so one decomposition has . Addition across degrees gives . The bounds are uniform over , which proves the assertion.  A leading lattice-point count alone permits isolated holes in every degree. An interior point here has at least possible decompositions, whereas only use a missing summand. The addition property then forces the point itself into .      Interpolation in an elongated simplex  The interpolation argument in and uses the Chinese remainder theorem in the last coordinate.    Let and be integers. Let be distinct, and define Then separates jets through degree at For , the tuple is empty and .    Write the coordinates as , with . A prescribed jet through degree at has a unique expression For each , the ideals are pairwise comaximal. Their product is generated by a monic polynomial of degree . The Chinese remainder theorem therefore gives a polynomial such that and .  The polynomial has all the prescribed jets. Every monomial in its -summand satisfies for every and . Hence Thus . The proof uses coefficients of powers of local parameters, so it applies in every characteristic.      Jets from additive exponent sets  A smaller simplex translated into the interior allows to be applied to the exponents in . This is and the interpolation part of .    Let and be integers, let , and let Suppose that finite sets , for every integer , satisfy and For every real and every choice of distinct , the space separates jets through degree at the points for all sufficiently large .    Choose with . Choose a vector satisfying Then . Choose with . For large , all translated simplices lie in a single compact subset of . By , for all sufficiently large .  Contraction and positive translation place the interpolation simplex in a compact subset of the interior. The interior-filling lemma applies there. The translation becomes a Laurent monomial factor. This factor is a unit at every torus point, so it gives an automorphism of each jet algebra.  Let . Since , we have for large . For every , Thus , and proves jet separation by the monomials with these exponents. Multiplication by the Laurent monomial of exponent induces an isomorphism of each jet algebra at a torus point, because this monomial is a unit there. The translated monomials belong to , which proves the required surjectivity.     "
